#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272036 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124578 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d22e4bf-1d84-587b-8106-e27319ecbc95" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "049cae38-d28e-50d2-9e31-bfce5568b1a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ is not diagonalizable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269310} {:answer/id #uuid "cd6c744e-908e-57a1-8cb1-20dafb2a4908" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 9\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271069} {:answer/id #uuid "dd2492a2-7015-5ded-b799-903daf045465" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 9\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271208} {:answer/id #uuid "e6608ae8-8bcd-5080-bdcc-c2571a84fbe6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -9\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271293} {:answer/id #uuid "f20d3c9f-a867-5095-a18b-5e476244d8be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -9\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271393}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dd2492a2-7015-5ded-b799-903daf045465" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 9\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271208} :answer-field/id #uuid "7115c4c6-c6f7-5cef-bbe2-99bae365672e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271709}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a841d0eb-a2cb-5e4b-9b90-23098b107a30" :question/math-academy-id "q-140261" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 & -4 \\\\ 2 & 9 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 9\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \nThe matrix $A$ has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=6,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=7,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=9.$\nFind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{-1}AP.$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we are already given the eigenvalues of the matrix: \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=6,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=7,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=9.$ \n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ Applying our usual method, we get the following.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=6,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-6I & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 & -4 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-6I)x=0$ gives us the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=7,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-7I & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -3 & -4 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-7I)x=0$ gives us the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{3}=9,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-9I & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -3 & -4 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-9I)x=0$ gives us the eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 9\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323891 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124533 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "05cb6509-dc1c-53b7-a495-4cfec2abdfe0" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether a Given Matrix Is Diagonalizable Over the Real Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bc2007e2-3435-5dff-a65a-f6f5c5d15506" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ is not diagonalizable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322606} {:answer/id #uuid "e2c5bebe-1930-54ba-93c1-94b60caab892" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322921} {:answer/id #uuid "f4022f4c-c327-5236-b35a-01eb5d5b4f1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323060} {:answer/id #uuid "f665fa4c-815b-56af-af58-2be052cb34ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323078} {:answer/id #uuid "fd056d48-6a05-537c-bcbb-00db56773cfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323142}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bc2007e2-3435-5dff-a65a-f6f5c5d15506" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ is not diagonalizable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322606} :answer-field/id #uuid "8dd75abe-eb94-5e7d-9544-859cf3e1a7ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323445}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1b01b4e3-a1f6-5e22-a9f4-2c6e0ea73a07" :question/math-academy-id "q-37256" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -6 \\\\ 14 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nDetermine whether $A=PD{P}^{-1}$ for some invertible matrix $P$ and a diagonal matrix $D,$ where $P$ and $D$ are both real. In the affirmative case, find a possible diagonal matrix $D.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to determine whether $D$ is diagonalizable over $R.$\n\nWe start by computing the eigenvalues of $A.$ To do that, we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 0 & -6 \\\\ 14 & 3-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & -6 \\\\ 3 & -5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )((2-\\lambda )(-5-\\lambda )+18) & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda +8) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the quadratic polynomial  $\\,{\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda +8\\,$ we have that the discriminant $D<0.$ So, it doesn't have real roots. \n\nTherefore, $A$ is not diagonalizable over $R.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382072 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124534 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0a6d3b3-be78-540d-bb10-47c7fff64001" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a Matrix Given Part of the Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d113fd3-c71b-5d9e-955d-e46183c3748e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382074} {:answer/id #uuid "39618bc3-2b12-586f-bdd9-ef421e79b6a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382075} {:answer/id #uuid "a3f72f3d-98cf-5349-9c0a-5e49707de3c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382076} {:answer/id #uuid "04d7ca2c-b383-5d9e-84b4-63fd717e6dc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382077} {:answer/id #uuid "708eb7f4-bd39-5814-8802-8b791bc777c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382078}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a3f72f3d-98cf-5349-9c0a-5e49707de3c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382076} :answer-field/id #uuid "271a716c-d9a8-5efe-8102-cf2499e7bd78" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382073}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1f806fd6-4180-5914-82ca-9edb27806cc1" :question/math-academy-id "q-37848" :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}8 & -4 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 12 & -10 & -6\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & a & -2 \\\\ -1 & b & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $A=PD{P}^{-1},$ find the value of $(a+b)^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagonal matrix $D$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=6,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=-1.$\n\nSo, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=6.$\n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{2}=6.$ Computing $A-6I,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{2}I & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & -4 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 12 & -10 & -6\\end{aligned}]-6[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & -2 \\\\ -3 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 12 & -10 & -12\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ that we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 12 & -10 & -12 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-6){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 14 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 14 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+2{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st  and the $2$nd ones). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation we obtain ${x}_{2}=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get  ${x}_{1}={x}_{3}.$\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{3} \\\\ 0 \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{3}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $(a+b)^{2}=(0+1)^{2}=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402575 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124578 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d22e4bf-1d84-587b-8106-e27319ecbc95" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "99584719-3306-525f-8ff1-816884430043" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402577} {:answer/id #uuid "91430aee-305c-5083-a738-7954753b326b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402578} {:answer/id #uuid "2955626a-1299-50b8-8b43-a19bf6c7b887" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402579} {:answer/id #uuid "34185d7e-94a1-519b-9e07-ed69ef1852ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402580} {:answer/id #uuid "c031ac61-6fa3-543a-8d72-e270fc225609" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402581}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "34185d7e-94a1-519b-9e07-ed69ef1852ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402580} :answer-field/id #uuid "97159dc8-c246-515e-a270-dbbd6b9b5ec3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402576}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "197a265b-1aed-5f18-a576-60c74ff63df1" :question/math-academy-id "q-37853" :question/problem "$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe matrix $B$ has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=4.$\nFind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{-1}BP.$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we are already given the eigenvalues of the matrix: \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=4.$ \n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ Applying our usual method, we get the following.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(B-2I)x=0$ gives us the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-(-2)I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(B-(-2)I)x=0$ gives us the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{3}=4,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-4I & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(B-4I)x=0$ gives us the eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432170 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124534 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0a6d3b3-be78-540d-bb10-47c7fff64001" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a Matrix Given Part of the Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ea81202-7482-5812-86cb-8eb7218d7d61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432153} {:answer/id #uuid "78bcd352-9548-567a-a122-5893ecb2b938" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432161} {:answer/id #uuid "94406db6-bcd3-5a97-9f39-575286530355" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432162} {:answer/id #uuid "b8490153-50bf-57a4-8199-293216881d4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432164} {:answer/id #uuid "ef768124-15c8-5064-8cfc-be6969ca32f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432167}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef768124-15c8-5064-8cfc-be6969ca32f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432167} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4aa1985-d36d-5245-9e3f-b2f24a2cdea0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432168}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "938c5c1e-3528-5126-9a4c-fc692c630173" :question/math-academy-id "q-40161" :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & ∗ \\\\ 0 & 1 & ∗ \\\\ 0 & 0 & ∗\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $D={P}^{-1}AP,$ find the third column of the matrix $P.$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagonal matrix $D$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=4.$ \n\nSo, the third column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{3}=4.$\n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{3}=4.$ Computing $A-(4)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{3}I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]-(4)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations whose augmented matrix $M$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above matrix is already  in row echelon form.  It has two pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable.  From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}={x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{2}(-4({x}_{3})-6{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =5{x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{3}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].$"}]}
